<section class="container page">
  <h1 class="page__title">Join us</h1>
  <p class="page__lede">The best way to understand our group is to look around the site. If what you see resonates, we would like to hear from you. We are hiring at all levels, and we value your character (curiosity, creativity, honesty, generosity, and a collaborative spirit) as much as your past experience. Below are the routes in; if none fits exactly but the work excites you, reach out anyway.</p>
  {% include "partials/page-submenu.njk" %}

  <div class="join-grid">
    <article class="join-card">
      <h2>PhD students</h2>
      <p>Fully funded departmental studentships and supervisor-led applications throughout the year.</p>
      <details class="join-details">
        <summary>More info</summary>
        <div class="join-detail">
          <h2>What we look for</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Bachelor's or master's in Engineering, Computer Science, or Applied Physics.</li>
            <li>Strong fundamentals in solid mechanics, with creativity and a passion for research.</li>
            <li>Programming proficiency (Python), and experience with FEM/FEA (Abaqus, ANSYS, COMSOL), CAD/AM, and/or robotics.</li>
            <li>Clear, proactive communication, and the ability to work both independently and in a team.</li>
            <li>Prior research experience and/or publications are a plus but not required.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>What we offer</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Immersive training in experimental mechanics, advanced manufacturing, and architected metamaterials, spanning experiment, computation, and theory.</li>
            <li>Access to world-class facilities, and a collaborative network across the College.</li>
            <li>Opportunities for conference travel, impactful publications, and cross-disciplinary projects.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>How to apply</h2>
          <p>There are two routes in. Each year the Department of Aeronautics runs a competitive round of fully funded PhD Studentships, covering fees and a tax-free stipend, with applications typically opening in the autumn and closing in early January. Outside that round, you can apply at any time through Imperial's online application system, naming your proposed supervisor and any funding you are seeking.</p>
          <p>Either way, email <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">Dr. Chen</a> first with your CV and a short note on why AIM Lab is a good fit for you.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </article>

    <article class="join-card">
      <h2>Postdocs</h2>
      <p>Funded positions and joint fellowship applications at the intersection of mechanics, materials, computation, and design.</p>
      <details class="join-details">
        <summary>More info</summary>
        <div class="join-detail">
          <h2>What we look for</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Ph.D. in Aerospace, Mechanical or Civil Engineering, Applied Physics or Mathematics, Computer Science, or a related field.</li>
            <li>Expertise in some of the following: computational design, experimental mechanics, architected materials, origami and kirigami, additive manufacturing.</li>
            <li>Strong publication record and excellent written and oral communication.</li>
            <li>Ability to work independently, lead projects, and mentor students.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>What we offer</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>A highly collaborative environment at the intersection of mechanics, materials, computation, and design.</li>
            <li>Access to state-of-the-art fabrication and testing (3D printing, imaging analysis, mechanical testing) and university HPC resources.</li>
            <li>Close collaboration across engineering, with opportunities to co-author high-impact publications and proposals.</li>
            <li>Competitive benefits per university guidelines, with strong mentoring and career development.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>How to apply</h2>
          <p>Email <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">Dr. Chen</a> with: (1) a cover letter describing your research fit and goals, (2) a CV with publications and contact information for two to three references, and (3) a critical review of one of our group's publications. Review is rolling until positions are filled.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </article>

    <article class="join-card">
      <h2>Undergraduate &amp; MEng</h2>
      <p>UROP placements and final-year projects for Imperial undergraduates.</p>
      <details class="join-details">
        <summary>More info</summary>
        <div class="join-detail">
          <h2>What we look for</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Curiosity, reliability, and a genuine interest in mechanics, materials, design, and art.</li>
            <li>Helpful skills: Python coding, CAD (Rhino3D), 3D printing, basic electronics and instrumentation, data analysis, or FEA tools.</li>
            <li>Coursework in mechanics or materials is beneficial.</li>
            <li>A time commitment of roughly 8 to 16 hours per week during term, or full-time in the summer.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>What you'll do</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Contribute to projects in architected materials, origami, or 3D printing and mechanical testing.</li>
            <li>Build reproducible tools (scripts, datasets, small libraries) and co-author posters or papers when appropriate.</li>
            <li>Take part through UROP, a final-year project, or summer research, with hourly compensation as funding allows.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>How to apply</h2>
          <p>Email <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">Dr. Chen</a> with: (1) a short paragraph on your interests and availability, (2) a CV or resume, and (3) an unofficial transcript. Applications are reviewed on a rolling basis as projects open.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </article>

    <article class="join-card">
      <h2>Visitors</h2>
      <p>Visiting students and researchers with their own funding are welcome for short or extended stays. Email at least three months ahead of your intended dates with a short description of what you would like to work on: <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">{{ site.email }}</a>.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>
